.SH NAME
ns_totp \- Returns a decimal-coded time-based one-time password (TOTP)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBns_totp\fR ?\fB-digest \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-digits \fIinteger\fR\fR? ?\fB-interval \fIinteger\fR\fR? ?\fB-user_id \fIinteger\fR\fR? ?\fB-key \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-time \fItime\fR\fR?
.sp
.BE
.SH DESCRIPTION The function returns a time-based one time password
(TOTP) as described in RFC-6238\&. The function uses the HOTP function
of RFC-4226 but adds the possibility to restrict the validity of the
password to a time window\&.
.PP
RFC-6238 defines TOTP as:
.CS


 TOTP = HOTP(K, T), where T is an integer

 K: key
 T: time slice (moving factor for one time passwd)

.CE
The implemented function ns_totp is a generalization by allowing to
specify the message digest algorithm, the number of characters of
the returned truncated value, a used_id and a time\&.
.PP
The listed command is available in installations when
NaviServer is compiled with OpenSSL support and where
the nsf package is installed\&.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBns_totp\fR ?\fB-digest \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-digits \fIinteger\fR\fR? ?\fB-interval \fIinteger\fR\fR? ?\fB-user_id \fIinteger\fR\fR? ?\fB-key \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-time \fItime\fR\fR?
.PP
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB-digest\fR \fIdigest\fR
Specifies the message digest algorithm used for checksum computation\&.
The available algorithms are provided by OpenSSL\&. Typical choices
include \fBsha1\fR, \fBsha256\fR, and \fBsha512\fR\&.  The
default is \fBsha256\fR\&.
.TP
\fB-digits\fR \fIdigits\fR
Specifies the number of digits in the generated one-time password
(OTP)\&. The default value, as recommended in the RFC, is \fB8\fR\&.
.TP
\fB-digits\fR \fIinterval\fR
Specifies the validity period of the OTP in seconds (time step size)\&.
The default value is \fB30\fR\&.
.TP
\fB-digits\fR \fIuser_id\fR
Specifies the user identifier for deriving a per-user secret\&.  The
secret can be provided explicitly with \fI-key\fR, or indirectly via
\fI-user_id\fR in combination with a configured server secret
(\fIserversecret\fR)\&. For testing, use \fI-key\fR; for production, use
\fI-user_id\fR to ensure each user has a personalized secret, seeded
via configuration (see configuration section)\&.
.TP
\fB-key\fR \fIkey\fR
Specifies the secret key used for generating the OTP (the "K" in the
RFC formula)\&. This is usually a decoded Base32 string\&.  If \fI-key\fR
is not provided, \fI-user_id\fR together with \fIserversecret\fR
must be used\&.
.TP
\fB-time\fR \fItime\fR
Specifies the time value (epoch seconds) used as the base for the OTP
computation\&. If omitted, the current system time is used\&.
.PP
.SH EXAMPLES
Minimal example:
.CS


 % ns_totp -key "tweedie123"
 33888450

.CE
.PP
A common application for TOTP is integration with authenticator
apps such as Google Authenticator, which expect a specially formatted URL:
.PP
\fBotpauth://TYPE/LABEL?PARAMETERS\fR
.PP
where TYPE is either \fBhotp\fR or \fBtotp\fR\&.
This URL is typically encoded in a QR code\&. Most authenticator apps can scan
such codes directly, or provide means to enter the parameters manually\&.
.PP
The URL encodes several parameters relevant for TOTP generation:
.IP \(bu
\fBsecret\fR: the Base32-encoded shared secret (mandatory)
.IP \(bu
\fBperiod\fR: validity interval in seconds, default \fB30\fR
.IP \(bu
\fBdigits\fR: number of digits in the OTP, default \fB6\fR
.IP \(bu
\fBalgorithm\fR: hash algorithm, default \fBSHA1\fR
.PP
Among these, the only mandatory parameter is the \fBsecret\fR\&.
When using \fBns_totp\fR, it is usually necessary to extract at least
\fBsecret\fR and \fBdigits\fR from the URL, and to provide
\fB-digest\fR explicitly, since the defaults of \fBns_totp\fR
(\fBsha256\fR and \fB8\fR digits) differ from those used by many
authenticator apps\&.
.CS


 # Decoded authenticator URL
 % set url otpauth://totp/IDENT?secret=XSECRETSECRETSECRETSECRETSECRETX&period=30&digits=6&issuer=WU%20Wien

 # Extract parameters from URL
 % set p [ns_set array [ns_parsequery [dict get [ns_parseurl $url] query]]]
 secret XSECRETSECRETSECRETSECRETSECRETX period 30 digits 6 issuer {WU Wien}

 # Generate TOTP
 % package require base32
 % ns_totp -digest sha1 -digits [dict get $p digits] -key [base32::decode [dict get $p secret]]
 259664

.CE
.SH CONFIGURATION
.CS


 ns_section "ns/server/server1" {
   # \&.\&.\&.
   ns_param serversecret "swordfish"
   # \&.\&.\&.
 }

.CE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ns_crypt, ns_hmac, ns_hotp, ns_md, ns_rand, ns_sha1, nsd
.SH KEYWORDS
TOTP, crypto, encoding, nsf
